course: dictionary
lesson: 8767
title: Sew: joining things with a needle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The verb sew means to join cloth together, or fix something like a button onto cloth, using a needle and thread. «Elizabeth Fry began visiting women prisoners and taught them to sew and read.» The words around sew, like read and taught, show it is a skill people learned.
explain: Look at «sew on a button» and «sew up a hole». Here sew means fix or mend, and the words after it tell you what is being fixed. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «Ella sewed up the hole in her coat» still makes sense if sew means mend.
explain: Sew has several forms: today I sew, yesterday I sewed, and I have sewn or sewed. A sewing machine does the same job faster. In everyday speech, sew up can also mean to finish something or make sure of it, as in «We have sewn up the match with three goals».
fact: Sew means to join cloth or attach things with a needle and thread.
fact: A sewing machine makes stitches for you by looping one thread round a second thread.
fact: The past forms of sew are sewed or sewn, so you can say «I have sewn» or «I sewed».
fact: Sewing needles are sharp, so keep your fingers well away from the needle.
fact: To sew up something informally means to finish it or make sure of it.
exercise: «Elizabeth Fry taught the women prisoners to sew and read.» What does sew mean here? | To wash clothes in a river | *To join cloth with a needle and thread | To cook a meal | To run very fast
exercise: «Ella had to sew on a button before school.» What is Ella doing to the button? | Throwing it away | Painting it | Counting its holes | *Fixing it on with a needle and thread
exercise: Choose the right form: «Yesterday, Sam ___ a bag for his dog.» | *sewed | sews | sewing | sew
test: The phrase «sew up a hole» means: | Throw the cloth away | Leave the hole open | Make the hole bigger | *Mend the hole with stitches
test: Finish the sentence: «Before the dance, Aisha has ___ her costume.» | *sewn | sewing | sews | sew
exam: In «We have sewn up the match with three goals», sewn up means: | *Made certain of winning | Stitched the ball together | Left the match open | Lost the match
